  <h1>Lemon Law by State</h1>
  <p><em>Reviewed by Aspen Lockwood (AL), Editor-in-Chief. Updated May 2026.</em></p>
  <h2>California (strongest protections)</h2><p>Song-Beverly Consumer Warranty Act. Coverage: 18 months or 18,000 miles from delivery. Repair attempts: 2 for safety defects, 4 for others, or 30 days out of service. Civil penalty up to 2× actual damages for willful violations. No mandatory arbitration before suing.</p>
  <h2>New York</h2><p>Coverage: 2 years or 18,000 miles. Repair attempts: 4 for same defect, or 30 days out of service. Strong attorney fee provision. One of the more plaintiff-friendly states in the Northeast.</p>
  <h2>Texas</h2><p>Coverage: 24 months or 24,000 miles. Must first go through manufacturer&rsquo;s dispute resolution program. Attorney fees available. More procedural hurdles than California or New York.</p>
  <h2>Florida</h2><p>Coverage: 24 months from delivery, or during the Lemon Law Rights Period (first 24 months). Repair attempts: 3 for same defect (with notice to manufacturer), or 30 days out of service. Mandatory arbitration in some cases.</p>
  <h2>Federal: Magnuson-Moss</h2><p>Available in all states as a supplement to state lemon law. Covers any product sold with a written warranty. Attorney fees mandatory for prevailing plaintiffs. No minimum repair attempt requirement — just a breach of warranty. Useful for used vehicles outside state lemon law periods.</p>
